2. Strategy to Reduce Script Execution Time
Looking at /home/ayush/Desktop/NIDAR/scripts/robust_square_flight.py, there are several programmatic bottlenecks we can optimize to make the drone fly the trajectory significantly faster:

A. Eliminate or Reduce Waypoint Hover Time

Current: Lines 142-146 force the drone to hover in place for 1.5 seconds at every single waypoint (hover_end = rospy.Time.now() + rospy.Duration(1.5)).
Optimization: Reduce this to 0.2 seconds or remove it entirely. This alone will shave off ~5-6 seconds from the flight time.
B. Increase Waypoint Acceptance Radius (Corner Cutting)

Current: Line 132 checks if the drone is within 0.45 meters of the waypoint before moving to the next one (if dist < 0.45:).
Optimization: Increase this threshold to 0.8 or 1.0. The drone will transition to the next leg of the square sooner, maintaining its momentum and rounding the corners instead of coming to a near-stop at each vertex.
C. Reduce Pre-flight Delays

Current: Line 74 pre-streams 50 setpoints at 20Hz to satisfy PX4's OFFBOARD requirements. This takes exactly 2.5 seconds.
Optimization: PX4 usually only requires about 10-20 setpoints to accept OFFBOARD mode. We can reduce the loop from range(50) to range(20), saving 1.5 seconds before takeoff.
Current: Lines 94 and 100 wait 1.5 seconds between retrying Arm and OFFBOARD commands.
Optimization: Reduce the retry cooldown from rospy.Duration(1.5) to rospy.Duration(0.5).
D. Increase Physical Drone Speed (Advanced) Currently, the script only publishes PoseStamped (target positions). The actual speed is dictated by PX4's internal parameters. To make the drone physically fly faster between points, you can:

Update PX4 Parameters: Change MPC_XY_CRUISE and MPC_XY_VEL_MAX to higher values in your PX4 configuration or via MAVROS parameter setting calls.
Use Velocity Setpoints: Instead of just sending positions, you could rewrite the loop to publish velocity vectors (geometry_msgs/TwistStamped to /mavros/setpoint_velocity/cmd_vel) pointing towards the next waypoint, giving you explicit programmatic control over the drone's speed.
